    "objid": "0002",
    "raw": "GROOM ONLY. Findings collected while executing todo:0101dd2c (LongSummary),\ntodo:71fabcb7 and todo:11038f9a. Each is an invariant the SKILL states and the\nAGENT must remember, where the TOOL could enforce, detect, or compute it. Every\none below was actually hit in a real session, not imagined.\n\n## 1. work-item-done sweeps unrelated work (hit TWICE)\n\n`work-item-done` commits with `git add -A`. If an agent edits ahead -- starts\nwork item N+1 before committing N -- both land in ONE commit, and the WorkItems\ntrail then records two items against one sha with one message describing only\nthe first. I did this twice in one session despite knowing about it, which is\nthe tell that discipline is the wrong mechanism.\n\nCandidates: warn when the dirty set includes files not plausibly part of the\ncursor item; accept a pathspec; or record the intended file set when an item\nbecomes the cursor and diff against it at completion. Even a bare warning would\nhave caught both instances.\n\nRelated: `--sha <HEAD>` on a clean tree is the escape hatch I used to record the\nsecond item against the shared commit. It works, but the resulting shape (two\nitems, one sha, a message describing one of them) is undocumented.\n\n## 2. `init --id <groom-id> --stay-on-parent` refuses on the wrong grounds\n\nThe guard at the top of `InitCommand.do()` is unconditional:\n\n    if read_todo_worktree(root) is not None:\n        raise TodoError(\"todo already exists on current branch; resume it instead of init\")\n\nIn PROMOTE mode with `--stay-on-parent`, the todo being promoted is named by\n`--id` and the current branch is irrelevant -- the flag exists precisely to say\n\"do not leave me on the new branch\". Yet standing on any branch that already has\na ticket blocks the promotion. Workaround used: create a detached-HEAD worktree\nat the intended fork point and promote from there.\n\nThis is arguably a defect rather than an offload, but it is the same lens: the\ntool is enforcing an invariant against the wrong subject (the checkout, not the\ntodo).\n\n## 3. Worktree lifecycle invariants are stated but never checked\n\nSKILL states hard rules: main checkout stays on master; a working todo is worked\nONLY in a linked worktree; every FINAL state (`done`/`merged`/`rejected`) implies\nno live worktree. Nothing verifies any of it. `ensure_worktree` is still a STUB.\nA todo left FINAL with a live worktree is an invariant violation that only a\nhuman notices.\n\n`doctor` already walks every todo and already reports repairs -- it could check\n`git worktree list` against todo states and report (or remove) orphans, and\ncould refuse/warn when a `working` todo is being edited from the main checkout.\n\n## 4. Already offloaded, working well (recorded so it is not re-litigated)\n\n`work-item-read`s `next` hint genuinely removed the \"what do I run now\" decision\n-- it names the mechanical command and the finish sequence. What remains with the\nagent there is real policy (split the item? spin a subtodo?), which is the right\nsplit. Cite this as the model the items above should follow: the TOOL emits\nmechanism as structured data, the SKILL keeps policy.\n\n## 4. --checkpoint happily creates a #6 violation\n\n`work-item-done --checkpoint` on the LAST open work item produces a done todo\nending in a checkpoint, which invariant #6 forbids -- `doctor` then hard-fails\nwith \"last work item is checkpoint\". The tool knows the item is last and knows\nthe rule, so it could refuse (or warn) at completion time instead of letting the\nagent discover it at the finish gate. Hit in todo:0101dd2c; repaired by\nre-sequencing WorkItems so the trail ends on the docs commit.\n\n## 5. Shared-commit nodes inherit the WRONG message (consequence of #1)\n\nWhen two items land in one commit, the second is recorded with `--sha HEAD` and\nINHERITS the first's commit message -- so the node's `message`, which SKILL calls\nthe durable per-step ledger entry, describes a different step. Concretely,\ntodo:0101dd2c WorkItems.3 (viewer) carried the doctor commit's message until it\nwas hand-corrected via set-json-path.\n\nThis makes #1 worse than \"two items share a sha\": the trail actively misdescribes\none of them, and the misdescription is silent. If the tool cannot prevent the\nsweep, it should at least refuse to inherit a message a previous node already\nclaims, or mark the node as sharing a commit.\n\n## Disposition rationale\n\nThe user asked that these be dropped if zero, folded into todo:6299408b if\ntrivial, and given their own todo if significant. Three concrete,\nindependently-actionable items -- two of which caused real damage in the session\nthat found them -- is significant."
  },
